From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@aeltra.eu>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 19:37:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] libfetch: do not truncate the chunk size in http_new_chunk()

io->chunksize is a size_t, but http_new_chunk() returns int and its
caller switches on the result to distinguish an error (-1) from the
terminating zero-length chunk (0).  The conversion is lossy on any
platform where int is narrower than size_t.

A chunk size that is a nonzero multiple of 2^32 truncates to 0 and is
taken for the terminating chunk: http_fillbuf() sets io->eof and returns
0, so the transfer is reported as a clean end of body and the remainder
is silently discarded.  A size whose low 32 bits are 0xffffffff yields
-1 and is reported as a read error instead.

io->chunksize already holds the parsed value, so return only a status
and let the caller test it for the terminating chunk.

Signed-off-by: Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@aeltra.eu>
---
 libfetch/http.c | 8 ++++----
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libfetch/http.c b/libfetch/http.c
index ebc94c3..0f9fc7c 100644
--- a/libfetch/http.c
+++ b/libfetch/http.c
@@ -145,7 +145,7 @@ http_new_chunk(struct httpio *io)
 	if (*p && *p != ';' && !isspace(*p))
 		return -1;
 
-	return io->chunksize;
+	return 0;
 }
 
 /*
@@ -194,11 +194,11 @@ http_fillbuf(struct httpio *io, size_t len)
 	}
 
 	if (io->chunksize == 0) {
-		switch (http_new_chunk(io)) {
-		case -1:
+		if (http_new_chunk(io) == -1) {
 			io->error = 1;
 			return (-1);
-		case 0:
+		}
+		if (io->chunksize == 0) {
 			io->eof = 1;
 			if (fetch_getln(io->conn) == -1)
 				return (-1);
